# Diagram templates for a synthetic invoicing system

Copy one block, keep the sentence that states its question, and rename nodes
to match the code. Each diagram stays under about 30 nodes.

## Context: what the system talks to

The invoicing service sits between customers, the payment provider and the
accounting team.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    accTitle: Invoicing system context
    accDescr: Customers and accountants use the invoicing service, which charges through a payment provider and sends e-mail.
    customer(["Customer"])
    accountant(["Accountant"])
    invoicing["Invoicing service"]
    payments[("Payment provider")]
    email[("E-mail provider")]
    customer -- "views and pays invoices (HTTPS)" --> invoicing
    accountant -- "issues invoices (HTTPS)" --> invoicing
    invoicing -- "charges cards (HTTPS API)" --> payments
    invoicing -- "sends receipts (SMTP)" --> email
```

## Container: deployable parts and protocols

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    accTitle: Invoicing containers
    accDescr: A web app calls the API; the API stores invoices and publishes events consumed by a worker.
    user(["Customer browser"])
    subgraph platform["Invoicing platform (trust boundary)"]
        web["Web app"]
        api["API service"]
        worker["Notification worker"]
        db[("Invoice database")]
        queue[["Event queue"]]
    end
    provider[("Payment provider")]
    user -- "HTTPS" --> web
    web -- "HTTPS JSON" --> api
    api -- "SQL" --> db
    api -- "publishes InvoicePaid" --> queue
    queue -- "delivers InvoicePaid" --> worker
    api -- "HTTPS API" --> provider
```

## Sequence: one payment request

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    accTitle: Paying an invoice
    accDescr: The API reserves the invoice, charges the provider with an idempotency key, then records the payment.
    actor Customer
    participant API as API service
    participant DB as Invoice database
    participant Provider as Payment provider
    Customer->>API: POST /invoices/{id}/payments (Idempotency-Key)
    API->>DB: load invoice, check state is issued
    alt invoice already paid
        API-->>Customer: 409 problem details
    else invoice issued
        API->>Provider: charge amount (same idempotency key)
        Provider-->>API: charge succeeded
        API->>DB: mark paid, store charge id
        API-->>Customer: 201 payment
    end
```

## Flow: deciding whether to send a reminder

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    accTitle: Reminder decision
    accDescr: Overdue unpaid invoices get a reminder unless one was sent in the last seven days.
    start(["Daily job"]) --> overdue{"Invoice overdue?"}
    overdue -- "no" --> skip(["Skip"])
    overdue -- "yes" --> paid{"Paid?"}
    paid -- "yes" --> skip
    paid -- "no" --> recent{"Reminder in last 7 days?"}
    recent -- "yes" --> skip
    recent -- "no" --> send(["Send reminder"])
```

## State: invoice lifecycle

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    accTitle: Invoice states
    accDescr: Drafts are issued, issued invoices are paid or voided, paid invoices can be refunded.
    [*] --> Draft
    Draft --> Issued: issue
    Issued --> Paid: payment succeeded
    Issued --> Voided: void
    Paid --> Refunded: refund
    Voided --> [*]
    Refunded --> [*]
```

## Entity relationship: stored data

```mermaid
erDiagram
    accTitle: Invoice data model
    accDescr: A customer has many invoices; an invoice has lines and payments.
    CUSTOMER ||--o{ INVOICE : "is billed by"
    INVOICE ||--|{ INVOICE_LINE : contains
    INVOICE ||--o{ PAYMENT : "is settled by"
    CUSTOMER {
        uuid id PK
        string display_name
    }
    INVOICE {
        uuid id PK
        uuid customer_id FK
        string state
        date due_date
    }
    INVOICE_LINE {
        uuid id PK
        uuid invoice_id FK
        decimal amount
    }
    PAYMENT {
        uuid id PK
        uuid invoice_id FK
        string provider_charge_id
    }
```
